  "image_name": "01a2c02bbdfb613d5bc154e9678339ef.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how C9orf72 repeat expansions disrupt RNA and protein handling in neurons, with many effects centered on the nucleus, nucleolus, and nuclear pore. The expanded repeat RNA forms nuclear foci that trap proteins, leading to protein sequestration and retention of RNA in the nucleus. Defects at the nuclear pore appear to impair nucleocytoplasmic trafficking, so RNAs and proteins are not properly exchanged between nucleus and cytoplasm. The repeat RNA is also linked to abnormal processing bodies and can be translated into dipeptide repeat proteins, which further feed into toxicity.\n\nAcross the cell, these disturbances connect to broader stress pathways. The nucleolus appears stressed, RNA splicing or editing is altered, and the endoplasmic reticulum and autophagy pathways are affected, with increased stress granules in the cytoplasm. The right side emphasizes a cycle in which both repeat RNA and dipeptide repeat proteins cause additional nuclear transport defects. It also suggests disrupted ribosome-related function, involving 40S and 60S subunits and a process labeled RANT. Overall, the image presents a network of interacting defects in RNA metabolism, protein homeostasis, and intracellular transport that together drive neuronal dysfunction.",
